	pages = {},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036990163?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Process Hazard Analysis (PHA) is vital for identifying and assessing industrial risks. The pre-causing step, where potential hazard causes are identified, is labor-intensive and demands specialized expertise. This study evaluates two technology-driven approaches to streamline this phase: the Pre-Causing Parser and an NLP-based system with chatbot integration. The Pre-Causing Parser automates cause generation by analyzing structured data on nodes, equipment, and systems. Engineers input files into the parser, which outputs a comprehensive list of potential causes for further refinement, and expediting preparation compared to manual methods. The NLP and Chatbot Automation approach employs Natural Language Processing and an interactive chatbot to assist engineers during pre-causing. Piping and Instrumentation Diagrams (P&IDs) are converted into structured formats, enabling NLP models to identify and suggest potential causes interactively. The chatbot answers queries, highlights relevant data, and aids in real-time scenario refinement, enhancing consistency in cause identification and reducing manual effort. Findings indicate that the Pre-Causing Parser is effective for rapid, high-level cause generation, suitable for projects requiring swift preparation. In contrast, the NLP and chatbot approach offers advanced predictive insights and interactive support, beneficial for complex analyses with dense data. This comparison highlights how these approaches can be tailored to specific project needs, providing insights for PHA practitioners aiming to optimize preparation processes through appropriate technological support. © 2025 GCPS. All Rights Reserved.},
